course: dictionary
lesson: 14779
title: Provoked: caused by something someone did
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: To provoke someone is to make them angry on purpose, or to cause a reaction. Provoked is the past form, so it tells you it has already happened. «The bear was provoked when the boys poked it with sticks.» The clue is poked with sticks: the boys caused the bear's anger.
explain: When provoked is used as an adjective, it describes something that was caused by someone's action. «The provoked giant roared and stamped.» The giant did not roar for no reason. Someone or something made him roar. Ask yourself: what came first?
explain: The opposite is unprovoked, which means with no reason. «The attack was unprovoked; nobody had touched the dog.» The word un- means not, so unprovoked means not caused by anything. Look for words like no reason or nobody had done anything to find the clue.
fact: Provoke means to make someone angry on purpose, or to cause a reaction.
fact: Provoked is the past form of provoke, and it can also describe something that was caused by someone's action.
fact: «Unprovoked» means with no reason, not caused by anything.
fact: In nature, most animal attacks are provoked, so the safest rule is to leave animals alone.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence: if «provoked» means caused by someone, does the sentence still make sense?
exercise: «The giant was provoked when the boys threw stones at him.» What does provoked mean here? | *Made angry on purpose | Fast asleep | Given a present | Lost in the fog
exercise: «The attack was unprovoked, and nobody knows why it happened.» What does unprovoked mean? | Happy and calm | *With no reason, not caused by anything | Very loud | Hidden in the trees
exercise: «The provoked bear charged across the field.» What caused the bear to charge? | *Someone or something made it angry | It was tired and hungry only | It wanted to sleep | The sun went down
test: «Mia did not touch the cat, but it still hissed and scratched her. Was the scratch provoked or unprovoked?» | Unprovoked, because it was raining | *Unprovoked, because Mia did nothing to make the cat angry | Provoked, because the cat was asleep | Provoked, because Mia did something to the cat
test: Which word is the past form of provoke? | *Provoked | Provocation | Provoke | Provokes
exam: Ben says, «The fox only attacked because Ben had kicked its den.» Which word best describes the attack? | Peaceful | *Provoked | Unprovoked | Accidental
